{"text": "WS Ye Suite 404, 22 Battery Street\nSan Francisco, California 96111\n\nCo-Chairmen: Houston |. Floumoy and John F. Henning\n\n{ Northern Headquarters Contacts Southern Headquarters Contacts \u00bb*:\nJack S. McDowell #S-19 Ed O'Connor 7 a\n: Patricia Cameron Mize, Mayers\nDick Woodward a a? (213) 641-2051 :\n\n(sis) 3923904 FOR INNEDIATE RELEASE\n\nSheriff John R. McDonald of San Mateo County today\nannounced his support of Californians for Common Sense,\nthe statewide organization opposing Proposition 5, the\n~.4,..Smoking prohibitions proposition on the November ballot.\n\u201cI find the proposed ballot proposition on smoking 3\n\u2018prohibitions to be unrealistic and costly,\" Sheriff McDonald\nsaid. .\n\"I am certain that the people of California, in light\nof their recent expression at the polls, are concerned with \u201c\nthe added burden of laws that cannot be realistically enforced,\"\nhe added.\nPeace officers, under general alw, would be required to\narrest anyone they see violating the new smoking prohibitions:\nA mandatory $50 fine for every violation is also required. Each .\nday on which a violation occurs would be a separate offense and\npunishable as such. gas\nA study by Economics Research Associates, a national _\nresearch firm, estimates the cost of signs (at every entrance\nto every governmental facility), enforcement, prosecution and\ncourt time would cost California taxpayers $43 million in the\nfirst year to comply.\nCo-chairmen of Californians for Common Sense, the\norganization campaigning for defeat of Proposition $, are\n\nCenter for Public Affairs; Katherine B. Dunlap, prominent\nconservationist and civic leader; John F. Henning, executive", "words": [{"text": "WS", "x0": 133, "y0": 25, "x1": 252, "y1": 114, "conf": 87.0}, {"text": "Ye", "x0": 298, "y0": 81, "x1": 308, "y1": 94, "conf": 0.0}, {"text": "Suite", "x0": 317, "y0": 88, "x1": 343, "y1": 97, "conf": 92.0}, {"text": "404,", "x0": 348, "y0": 89, "x1": 369, "y1": 98, "co